    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Each team plays 3 games with each of the other 8 teams.\nIf we consider Team A playing with the 8 other teams, it plays 8 games.\nSince each game is played between two teams, each game is counted twice in this calculation.\nTherefore, the total number of games played is 8*3 / 2 = 12.\n\nEach triangle has length equal angles of 30 degrees. A line spells triangle makes on the diagram the interior angle of 30 degrees. How many possible ways can it spell such a boundary?\n\nGiven the problem, every possible path within a 7 by 7 grid containing a 30 degree turn is being considered. A simple counting strategy involves starting with all possible paths originating from any cell in the grid. For each point, 7 paths to vertices share a 30 degree turn. Therefore, no matter where we start, we add 7 paths until exiting the grid.\n\nWe should first account for the number of rows and columns (r and c respectively), and the total paths that can be taken from any vertex without encountering a boundary (r-1) & (c-1). For every cell inside the matrix, we can trace 7 paths, so initially, the total number of paths is (r-1)*(c-1)*7. However, care must be taken before the first row or column intersection as such a path terminates.\n\nFor the first row and column, we start eliminating the terminating cases. Thus, for the first row, this reduction adds (c-1)*7 to the count, while for each column, it involves subtracting 6.\n\nTherefore, the total number of paths through the area can be computed via:\n\n1. Initialization: (n(L-2)*(N-2)*7)\n2. Correction for Top row: (n(L-2)*6)\n3. Correction for Left column: (n(L+1)*(N-3)*7)\n4. Elimination of the terminating situations: (n(L-2)*n(N-3)*6)\n\nUpon execution of this algorithm, the function returns \"n\", which is the total number of distinct paths through a 7 by 7 matrix that contain a 30 degree turn.",
